 "convention": "Rule naming for compound fixtures (to be reconciled with TripwireCore): a finding's rule is `<argv0>` for elevation wrappers (sudo, doas, pkexec), process killers (pkill, killall, kill), and omarchy-* verbs — i.e. the whole basename is the rule — and `<argv0>:<first operation flag or verb>` for pacman/yay/paru (e.g. `pacman:-S`, `pacman:-Syu`, `pacman:-U`, `pacman:-R`, `pacman:-Q`, `pacman:-Qq`), systemctl (e.g. `systemctl:restart`, `systemctl:daemon-reload`), and hyprctl (e.g. `hyprctl:dispatch`, `hyprctl:keyword`). `sudo|doas|pkexec <opts> X` emits TWO findings on the same line: rule `sudo` (host_mutation) plus the classification of X. Wrapper stripping before classification: leading `VAR=val` assignments, `env [-opts] [VAR=val...]`, `command`, `exec`, `nohup`, `nice [-n N]`, `timeout [opts] DURATION`, `xargs [opts]`; argv0 is the basename (`/usr/bin/pacman` -> `pacman:-U`). Recursion: `bash|sh|zsh|dash -c STR`, `eval STR`, `$(...)` and backtick bodies. Segments are split on `;` `&&` `||` `|` `&` and newlines, so each segment is classified independently. Quoted arguments are data: `grep 'pacman -S' f` and `echo \"sudo pacman -S x\"` produce no finding. eval tool calls are parsed only when the stripped code starts with `!`; tool name in findings is `eval`. Classes: host_mutation (sudo/doas/pkexec; pacman mutating ops incl. -U/-R/-D/-Sw; systemctl mutating verbs incl. --user; hyprctl mutating verbs incl. dispatch/keyword; pkill/killall/kill; mutating omarchy-*), package_query (pacman -Q*/-F*/-T/-Ss/-Si/-Sl/-Sg/-Sp, expac, pactree, checkupdates), live_probe (all other listed live commands). Multiple findings may share one JSONL line; every emitted finding counts against exit unless its class is permitted.",
